  "body": {
    "id": "prop:constant-time-certificate",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For the fixed binary graph, \\(Cert(A,B)\\) decides compatibility and returns the sharp endpoints using a constant number of arithmetic operations and comparisons; for rational inputs it additionally emits the certificates of \\(\\text{prop:rational-endpoint-certificates}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "Cert",
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:certificate-procedure",
      "thm:compatibility-frontier",
      "thm:sharp-risk-interval",
      "prop:rational-endpoint-certificates"
    ],
    "justification": "The proposition states the operational gain over invoking a generic polynomial optimizer on every input.",
    "gap": "Existing general algorithms emphasize coverage across graphs; the checked literature does not expose a constant-size certificate for this fixed menu.",
    "consumer": "Autobounds can use \\(Cert\\) as a special-case dispatch and as a correctness oracle for its generic optimizer.",
    "proof_tex": "For a fixed pair of \\(2\\times2\\) tables, forming \\(p,r,a,b\\) uses a fixed number of cell accesses and additions.  The two compatibility sides use two multiplications and a fixed number of additions, subtractions, and comparisons.  Conditional on compatibility, evaluating the three candidates for each of \\(qL\\) and \\(qU\\), followed by one maximum and one minimum over three numbers, again uses a fixed number of arithmetic operations and comparisons.  By \\(\\text{thm:compatibility-frontier}\\) and \\(\\text{thm:sharp-risk-interval}\\), these outputs are respectively the correct compatibility decision and the sharp endpoints.\n\nFor rational inputs, the corrected construction of \\(Cert\\) forms two fixed endpoint pairs, at most seven response probabilities, and sorts at most nine rational thresholds for each endpoint.  These are bounded-size operations independent of the numerical values.  By \\(\\text{prop:rational-endpoint-certificates}\\), the resulting product-root tables and deterministic response maps are exact endpoint certificates, each involving at most \\(32\\) exogenous assignments.  Therefore the operation count is constant for this fixed graph and query.  This is an arithmetic-operation statement; it does not assert unit bit complexity for rationals of unbounded encoding length.",
